  "findings": [
    {
      "id": "SYN-008",
      "location": "src/claude/liturgy/roman-rite/1962/propers/temporal/58-eighteenth-after-pentecost/sections/concise/04-themes.tex lines 8-9 (PDF p.3, the opening thesis: 'and confesses in its single prayer that without God we cannot please God'), with lines 37-38 (p.3: 'It is the Mass's only oration.')",
      "problem": "The first sentence of the Themes says the Mass has a 'single prayer', but the same paragraph closes on 'a prayer to be made worthy of what has been received' (the Postcommunion). The page-1 map also lists the Secret and Postcommunion as prayers, and page 4 says the Mass 'ends its prayers with thanks'. The study's rubrical sense is exact: 'The Mass has one Collect' and 'a single oration of each kind' (sections/00-opening.tex). 'Only oration' is also the study's own usage (sections/05-appointed-texts.tex, 50-comparison.tex), and the page-1 note 'one oration of each kind' explains it. But 'single prayer' in plain English says something false, and an adult lay reader meets it in the thesis sentence. No argument rests on it, and the Collect is plainly the prayer meant.",
      "required_result": "Name the Collect in the thesis, for example 'and confesses in its Collect that without God we cannot please God' or 'in its one Collect'. For the same reason, line 38 could read 'It is the Mass's only collect'. Leave the rest of the paragraph unchanged.",
      "severity": "advisory"
    }
  ],
